A mathematician is uniquely qualified to say why anyone would give a shit about any of these results










I very much dislike openai's view that when results are obtained via processes involving sufficiently large proprietary RL models, the academic norm should be to leave all details of the process except the involvement of a proprietary model undisclosed and to leave most researchers involved unnamed. I've seen it suggested that those who take issue with OpenAI's handling of their recent mathematical results ought to specify what OpenAI should have done; very well: they should publish with full transparency on the processes by which they achieve their results, and their researchers should be putting their names on those publications.

They have insane amounts of compute, proprietary data, and human research talent. Once the model capabilities presently only available to them become generally available, their norm of assigning all credit to the LLM will become increasingly obviously untenable and deranged, and the effort involved in setting up systems em{around} LLMs of that caliber to obtain such results as they've obtained will become more visible.

Of course, by that time they will have even better models than that so they'll just keep doing the same shit, trying to pass off a massive material advantage in compute resources, data, and highly skilled intellectual laborers as something fundamentally different in kind from all other research.

also the lean certs are the main results, not the generated preprints, especially not the preprints that not only lack lean certs but that nobody has meaningfully reviewed. those "preprints" should be released as attachments/supporting material alongside actually-well-written writeups of their full process for creating their internal model as well as their full process for obtaining the mathematical results they obtained with it.






"maybe math isn't beautiful" is a bizarre reaction to the recent ai math stuff, imo. like i don't know how someone who previously genuinely thought math was beautiful could stop thinking so on the basis of those results; none of what i find beautiful in math has been lost or at all tarnished
like sure i don't find those particular results beautiful. there's a lot of theorems i don't find particularly beautiful or interesting in math. i don't think very many people who have any sense for "mathematical beauty" were ever of the opinion that every theorem provable in lean is beautiful


so one of the answers im hearing to this is along the lines that "ugly" ai results are indicative that humanity has been sort of "limited" by aesthetic taste, simply *unwilling* to go find all the answers to open problems bc we just weren't willing to suppose that the answers might be inelegant
however i don't buy this, bc these results are actually very much in line w/ the kind of stuff ppl have been churning out for a while. galactic runtime algos, bounds proofs w/ nasty arbitrary constants, &c &c are nothing new. & i also don't think the sheer quantity of them to be found is surprising
that kind of problem has basically been something you can go grind against and have a decent guarantee you'll come across some kind of result, inelegant as it might be. oai's results i guess just shine a light on how much of contemporary academic math is just "go swing a pickaxe in the results mine"




i guess being personally not part of academia and viewing "math" as something broader than "churn out papers" makes it easier for me to view the ripping apart of those ore-rich hills by a bucket-wheel excavator as something less than apocalyptic


anyway the "ore" in this metaphor is already something of questionable value anyway & the "publish or perish" grind is just one case of the broader societal problem of valuing nothing but the production of endless slop & requiring ppl to waste their lives doing pointless drudgery to pay the rent
the responses of academic mathematicians are then the panicked appeals of mine workers being asked "ok so if we can just roll up w/ a big machine... why shouldn't society just discard you all, toss you all in a landfill"
tho i think there's much more to math than the mining, the main reason we as a society even buy the "ore" of endless publications is not bc there's anyone out there that actually has a use for that ore in the way industries use ore irl, rather we pay for the ore as a proxy for funding math bc there
has to be some kind of "product" to satisfy the ideological conceit our economy has where everyone has to be "productive" all the time in some legible way, even if that productivity isn't doing anyone much good, or else deserves to be left out on the street to die of exposure in the winter


