excerpts from a convo w/ a friend; TODO convert this into a few clean paragraphs:


lots of like. long convoluted proofs from various areas

all building on pretty established topics

just kinda treating known "open problems" as a checklist to methodically grind thru, & treating a lean verified proof as all it takes to make a result adequate

alongside pdf writeups that explain the results poorly and don't always make the same exact argument the lean formalization makes

even if they argue to the same conclusion

& it's like

this is nothing like what math is to me lmao

it's very pathological

malicious compliance vibe

like "come up with any objective standard for work in your field and i will make a machine that performs that perfectly, while also clearly missing the actual point"

i guess mathematicians in academia have their livelihoods on the line when it comes to how the cultural conversation about it shakes out so a strong reaction there makes sense

but when it comes to like. what it means for math writ large, yea i think this is like. underwhelming stuff


it's kinda about "what is the actual point of proving things anyway"

like

the point of a proof is to communicate an understanding

that's prior to even formalization

formal systems make it so we can like. agree on what the rules of reasoning are in a particular theoretical context, so we don't have to hash out whether the axiom of choice holds, we can just say "this result is dependent on it" and agree that it is fine to invoke in this proof

but formal systems also have the side effect where

you can make a huge gnarly proof and just check each step without having to understand the entire thing all at once

and like. you can do that without formalization as well

but

formalization lets you communicate something like that precisely

but massive machine generated proofs are more like

someone looking at that whole process of like "here are the local rules of reasoning and here's the step by step process that proves some interesting result there" and thinks "ah ok so all that matters is that the steps in this formalism check out. well then i can just make a machine that checks that stuff and then nobody needs to care about reasoning any more"

it seems like malicious compliance with the formalization of reasoning, imo

100% following the letter of the law but completely contemptuous of the spirit of it

bc the point of proofs is to actually convince ourselves and one another of various facts about what precise ideas entail each other under various ideas of entailment and various foundational assumptions

but these oai proofs are like. not only failing to communicate any understanding, but produced also without understanding

and they're treating "there's a lean cert that checks out" as a standard for the quality of papers

which is just not really the standard in mathematics lol

what a lean cert checking out demonstrates is that insofar as we trust an engineered piece of software, we can be assured that the theorem encoded in lean follows from the axioms specified, according to the argument encoded in lean

but there are several ways that can fail to correspond with what's in the paper

the paper can be making a totally different argument for the same conclusion

what a lean cert checking out demonstrates is that insofar as we trust an engineered piece of software, we can be assured that the theorem encoded in lean follows from the axioms specified, according to the argument encoded in lean

& aside from issues of correspondence w/ the paper, this also has the issue of like. presenting math as though it all proceeds from one single foundational system

which is a bit of brainrot that's been afflicting the culture for some time

usually ppl treating zfc set theory as the true foundation

when like

the history of foundationalism is a farce imo

19th/20th century epistemic foundational aspirations got dunked thru the fucking floor

by theorems proving that reasonably powerful formalisms can't guarantee they have all the nice properties one would want the One True Foundation to have and also by that foundational sort of philosophy being trash more broadly

but the foundationalists just kinda shrugged and decided "who cares let's just declare zfc the foundation of all math" and the meme has survived

the funny thing is tho

lean isnt even that

lean is built on a totally different foundation

dependent type theory

which is

galaxy brain 5d alien bullshit

i mean

i mean "galaxy brain 5d alien bullshit" somewhat affectionately

but

it's also

very new

has the same epistemic/philosophical issues as any foundational project

so even w/ it being true that "any proof verified in lean, if the verification is done properly, has a very very very high probability that the theorem as-stated-in-lean indeed holds as a consequence of the axioms used"

it's still not the case that having a lean cert to go along w/ a paper is "the gold standard" in math

most mathematical work doesn't even explicitly proceed from any foundational system's axioms

ppl proceed from a variety of formalisms or even informal arguments

and it's all legitimate math

like u can lay out the axioms of group theory w/o grounding them in the axioms of a particular axiomatization of set theory

"does this result depend on axiom of choice" is less a question about the true nature of a proof as written and more a question about what happens if you try to encode that proof in zfc

so like i would say "most papers don't invoke choice" & a zfc nerd might be like "uhm actually it assumed all vector spaces have a basis and that's equivalent to choice" & it's like

that's true if you try to like. re-encode the proof in your preferred foundations

you would end up needing choice to encode that particular move of the proof in set theory, sure

but at the same time

the proof itself didn't actually rely on any such foundation and didn't *need to* to be rigorous and valid

rigor is not the same thing as formalization for one thing

but that's i guess too spicy a take for the 21st century on this chud planet

and furthermore

even if the proof assumed every vector space has a basis

it might not actually *need* the strongest version of that to still hold

bcl ike

most vector spaces anybody gives a shit about have a basis even if u dont assume choice


a fun thing re: "rigor" & formalization

is

formalizations of calculus

the main way we formalize calculus & real analysis today was developed originally as a way of "making it rigorous" bc ppl were very anxious about whether calculus was actually sufficiently rigorous

bc there was a lot of argument by intuitions abt infinitesimals for example

or other things

and like

now we have these ideas of epsilon-delta proofs about limits &c &c

but

in the long run

actual formalizations have been developed for a lot of the shit that was previously discarded as an "unrigorous intuition"

like there has been a formalization of calculus in terms of infinitesimals

umbral calculus too

&c &c

a lot of math that was dismissed as the "unrigorous intuition" that needed to be replaced w/ a properly rigorous formalization in some "more foundational" system turned out to be just as formalizable in the long run

it just didn't track w/ the formalization that ppl at the time were most taken with

didn't fit nicely into the conceptual schemes that were held in highest regard

in an obvious and easy way

and ppl mistook "fits into this foundational mindset" as being what it meant for a mathematical result to be properly rigorously established but like

that just wasn't the reality!!

math is pluralistic

& the especially galling thing is that that notion of rigor is like

one that shits all over the ppl that actually originally came up w/ calculus lmfao

being like "ah they're just mystical hacks guessing"

no

they were thinking in a way that actually does hold up!!


