quote from sanderson on tao's blog:
"One clear shortcoming of a motivated explanation is that its validity is not binary the way a proof’s is. This is a big reason proof is so useful a way to measure progress: You can clearly define what does and does not have a proof yet. There will never be Lean for motivated explanations."

this is not a shortcoming of motivated explanation. this is a shortcoming in the ideological expectation that everything should be evaluable by "objective" binary judgements






from thurston's proof & progress essay:
"Basic concepts used every day within one subfield are
often foreign to another subfield. Mathematicians give up on trying to understand
the basic concepts even from neighboring subfields, unless they were clued in as
graduate students."
this is something i think AI can help quite a bit to address!









the oai proofs are basically empirical data for ppl to pick apart & theorize about if they are interested in doing so; math, in its pluralistic nature, need not change anything about itself to encompass such pursuits

arguably such pursuits belong more to computer science than to mathematics, but i'm of the opinion that compsci is a subfield of math anyway



maybe cut or reduce this bit, it is slightly breaking the vow

I've made a vow never to make a long-form post about vibecoding, but this post is about AI more broadly so I feel it's fine to say a few words on it: How much benefit is there, really, in getting swept up in feelings of grandiose potential all while you have to keep paying 200 dollars a month to companies run by reactionary billionaires in order to feel able to continue work on your software projects? As far as I can see there hasn't been any corresponding explosion in the quantity of good software out there. It often seems as if those experiencing the feelings of power and potential forgotten that people could already do impressive things with code em{before} AI hit the scene. Software engineers have always been quick to become die-hard evangelists for new tools and much of the talk about LLMs has the same character as can be seen in other SWE fads of the past and present.

On the other hand you have the $scare{AI is never good for anything} types. I think that's also a ridiculous position. There are plenty of things AI is useful for. Even the free tier models are useful.

A lot of people tie their identity up in either their embrace or rejection of AI, and I think that's a tendency one ought to make a conscious effort to overcome in either case.











Formalization isn't em{what this process of proof $scare{really is,}} it's em{an attempt at precisely specifying it}. Formal systems have properties that make them extremely useful in mathematics, especially when it comes to communicating rigorous proofs; formal systems are themselves a subject of mathematical inquiry, and learning about them is vital to a mathematical education. However they are not the foundation of all mathematical practice. They are em{especially} unsuited to form an epistemological foundation, for reasons both philosophical and technical.


Now my point isn't $scare{you really should believe that the square root of two is irrational and really shouldn't believe the OAI N-S proof}, rather it's to gesture at there being a rich world of epistemic and phenomenological diversity among the things we call $scare{proofs}.






you can encode group theoretic axioms in zfc, that doesn't mean that group theorists are all "using zfc", any more than it means they're all using any other foundation that could encode their work.








If we had models being trained basically exactly the same way as OpenAI's but the opt-in $scare{train on my shit} checkbox was off by default and users could have a greater regulatory assurance it was even being respected, and this training was being done by an institution controlled by the public, accessible as a public service, it'd actually be uncomplicatedly cool as hell. Everything everyone is working on instantly absorbed into a common pool of knowledge accessible to all, as fast as they can develop their ideas. That's utopian! What's not utopian is it being beholden to Sam Altman and shareholders, a private company inclined to try to compete with its users and claim credit for what is effectively a collective achievement. It's dystopian that access to such a system is gated behind a pricey subscription model or even pricier business-to-business bullshit, it being endlessly marketed as the soon-to-be replacement for all intellectual labor, or perhaps em{all labor}, and as having transcended through scale alone its nature as an RL-tuned LLM, as having undergone the mysterious phenomenon of $scare{emergence} and become something ineffably em{more} (but no, we're not going to make that rigorous) -- is just tedious!




clarifying "brittleness" vs effectiveness of transfer
RL brittleness i think takes on a different character when it comes to massively-scaled models w/ strong pretraining. but it's still there. i think a lot of RL-flavored brittleness is visible in strongly-RL'd models today




a take ive seen is that having the answers handed to you takes something important out of math. and while i think there's an element of truth in some formulations of the idea, i think that needs to be balanced against a frank acknowledgement that almost all the answers in math are handed to us already and that's great actually. i was born into a world where math is a mature field of study. there is too much already-discovered math for me to ever learn! my entire formal education in math consisted of people more or less directly handing me answers to questions i might never have even thought to ask. maybe truly independently i could have intuited some basic geometry. but the body of mathematics took millennia to assemble. and i love it when i find my questions already answered! though usually in such cases there is more than just an answer, and i think that's important. i don't think i would find it very satisfying to just get a conclusion and its Lean cert; that feels like a dead end to inquiry: "yes, it's proved, the proof is a massive library of Lean produced by an optimization routine, you'll never be able to make heads or tails of it". as with the earlier talk of malicious compliance with the concept of proof, i feel there's significantly less value to me in a proof where all i can get out of it is "the formalization exists, and lean says it checks out".




if i wanna be a real freak about it i could draw on society of the spectacle or maybe baudrillard

"wherever representation takes on an independent existence, the spectacle reestablishes its rule"



llms and ai

draw a distinction btwn llms (most of what this article has talked abt so far) and the cultural technology of the em{concept} of AI; a multifaceted cultural technology

orientation toward / against ai as identity-formation technology; even those who "never use ai" use the AI-concept to define themselves to others;

AI-concept as *brand*
AI-concept as spectacle

we really do have debates about the facts on the ground of the digital technologies tied up in the ai-concept; but to what degree are the positions we stake out em{truly about} the facts on the ground? it is a case by case judgement, and ambiguous.
one whose belief formation is, to the degree they can manage to control it, kept in the epistemic register and not the transactional register will find that the strongest advocates and enemies of AI are often ridiculous; one is repelled from the corresponding positions. but the positions are often not the point. "i don't trust any code written with ai" and "i don't trust any code written without ai" are ways of characterizing oneself along the lines "i am someone who cares about correctness and safety", just to two different audiences, and perhaps inflected with "i am meticulous and have a deep understanding of my craft, i have exacting standards and refined taste. accept no substitute" versus "i am quick to adapt, i stand at the bleeding edge and i can see what's coming, tomorrow everyone's going to be doing things the way i do them today".

surely i display some degree of this myself, even if it is more difficult to spot in my own thinking than in that of others. much of this post could probably be understood as an act of carving out an identity in relation to AI. but i'll stop there and keep the indulgent navel-gazing to myself.


aside or footnote on the transactional register of belief:

suspiciously-convenient "true believers" flirting on the edge of dishonesty as with religious belief; one believes what one must believe as a price for some benefit. and this can be indistinguishable from genuine belief even from "inside" one's mind, all while the cynical processing happens outside one's attention; we have no inner panopticon, structurally. our minds make moves in the dark. this is often the nature of religious beliefs but the phenomenon is more broad than that.

to understand the transactional register of belief as distinct from the epistemic is necessary to not be totally at a loss when encountering it! if you take all belief as epistemic you must write most people off as profoundly incompetent thinkers. yes, people really do believe all that magical shit about jesus, but that's in most cases transactional belief, it operates differently, *forms and maintains itself differently* from the belief that the hot stove will burn your hand and if you let go of a rock it will fall to the ground under the influence of gravity.

transactional beliefs often "pay more rent" than epistemic ones. transactional belief keeps one in the circles one wants to be in, including in situations of material dependency. being able to disbelieve is a privelege; not everyone can afford it, or at least the price is steep enough that it would effect a drastic change in their quality of life. the most positive vision of atheism is that everyone should have the privelege that if they were to disbelieve they would not lose so much of what they need. this is tied up in the abolition of material conditions of hierarchical interpersonal dependency




